  1218: 
  1219: 5.2 Signal descriptions
  1220: 
  1221: 5.2.1 CS0- (CHIP SELECT 0)
  1222: 
  1223: This is the chip select signal from the host used to select the Command Block Registers. See table 7.
  1224:      NOTE 3 - This signal has also been known in the industry as CS1FX-.
  1225: 
  1226: 5.2.2 CS1- (CHIP SELECT 1)
  1227: 
  1228: This is the chip select signal from the host used to select the Control Block Registers. See table 7.
  1229:           NOTE 4 - This signal has also been known in the industry as CS3FX-.
  1230: 
  1231: 5.2.3 DA2, DA1, and DA0 D( EVICE ADDRESS)
  1232: 
  1233: This is the 3-bit binary coded address asserted by the host to access a register or data port in the device.
  1234: See table 7.
  1235: 5.2.4 DASP- (Device active, device 1 present)
  1236: This is a time-multiplexed signal which indicates that a device is active, or that Device 1 is present. This
  1237: signal shall be an open collector output and each device shall have a 10K ohm pull-up resistor.
  1238: During power on initialization or after RESET- is negated, DASP- shall be deasserted by both Device 0 and
  1239: Device 1 within 1 ms, and then Device 1 shall assert DASP- within 400 ms, to indicate that Device 1 is
  1240: present.
  1241: Device 0 shall allow up to 450 ms for Device 1 to assert DASP-.
  1242: 
  1243: 10
  1244:                                                                                                                X3T10/0948D Revision 4c
  1245: 
  1246: DASP- shall be negated following acceptance of a command by Device 1 or after 31 s, whichever comes
  1247: first.
  1248: 
  1249: Any time after negation of DASP-, either device may assert DASP- to indicate that a device is active.
  1250: 
  1251: If the host connects to the DASP- signal for the illumination of an LED or for any other purpose, the host shall
  1252: ensure that the signal level seen on the ATA interface for DASP- shall maintain Voh and Vol compatibility,
  1253: given the Ioh and Iol requirements of the DASP- device drivers.
  1254: 
  1255: 5.2.5 DD0-DD15 (Device Data)
  1256: 
  1257: This is an 8- or 16-bit bi-directional data interface between the host and the device. The lower 8 bits are
  1258: used for 8-bit transfers e.g., registers, vendor specific bytes associated with the READ LONG and WRITE
  1259: LONG commands and, if the device supports the Features register capability to enable 8-bit-only data
  1260: transfers (see 8.24, Set features).
  1261: 
  1262: 5.2.6 DIOR- (Device I/O read)
  1263: 
  1264: This is the read strobe signal from the host. The falling edge of DIOR- enables data from a register or data
  1265: port of the device onto the signals, DD0-DD7 or DD0-DD15. The rising edge of DIOR- latches data at the
  1266: host and the host shall not act on the data until it is latched.
  1267: 
  1268: 5.2.7 DIOW- (Device I/O write)
  1269: 
  1270: This is the Write strobe signal from the host. The rising edge of DIOW- latches data from the signals, DD0-
  1271: DD7 or DD0-DD15, into a register or the data port of the device. The device shall not act on the data until it
  1272: is latched.
  1273: 
  1274: 5.2.8 DMACK- (DMA acknowledge) (optional)
  1275: 
  1276: This signal shall be used by the host in response to DMARQ to initiate DMA transfers.
  1277: 
  1278: NOTE 5 - This signal may be negated by the Host to suspend the DMA transfer in process. For Multi-Word
  1279: DMA transfers, the Device may negate DMARQ within the tL specified time (refer to figure 12) once
  1280: DMACK- is asserted and reassert it again at a later time to resume the DMA operation. Alternatively, if the
  1281: device is able to continue the transfer of data, the device may leave DMARQ asserted and wait for the host
  1282: to reassert DMACK-.
  1283: 
  1284: 5.2.9 DMARQ (DMA request) (optional)
  1285: 
  1286: This signal, used for DMA data transfers between host and device, shall be asserted by the device when it is
  1287: ready to transfer data to or from the host. The direction of data transfer is controlled by DIOR- and DIOW-.
  1288: This signal is used in a handshake manner with DMACK- i.e.; the device shall wait until the host asserts
  1289: DMACK- before negating DMARQ, and re-asserting DMARQ if there is more data to transfer.
  1290: 
  1291: This line shall be released (high impedance state) whenever the device is not selected or is selected and no
  1292: DMA command is in progress. When enabled by DMA transfer, it shall be driven high and low by the device.
  1293: 
  1294: When a DMA operation is enabled, IOCS16-, CS0- and CS1- shall not be asserted and transfers shall be 16-
  1295: bits wide.
  1296: 
  1297:      NOTE 6 - In ATA-1 devices, this signal was either totem-pole or tri-state in different
  1298:      implementations. In EISA systems, 5.6K pull-down is used to cause a logic low on undriven lines.
  1299:      ATA-2 defines this line to be in high-impedance mode except when DMA transfer is active from the
  1300:      selected device.
  1301: 
  1302:                                                                                                                                               11
  1303: X3T10/0948D Revision 4c
  1304: 
  1305: In systems which may use mixed devices where totem-pole drivers are used, and the system shares this line
  1306: with other non-ATA devices, the ATA host or ATA adapter shall ensure that appropriate protection is
  1307: employed to protect ATA device DMARQ drivers from damage.
  1308: 
  1309: 5.2.10 INTRQ (Device interrupt)
  1310: 
  1311: This signal is used to interrupt the host system. INTRQ is asserted only when the device has a pending
  1312: interrupt, the device is selected, and the host has cleared the nIEN bit in the Device Control register. If the
  1313: nIEN bit is equal to one, or the device is not selected, this output is in a high impedance state, regardless of
  1314: the presence or absence of a pending interrupt.
  1315: 
  1316: The interrupt pending condition shall be cleared by:
  1317:    - assertion of RESET-; or
  1318:    - the setting of the SRST bit of the Device Control register; or
  1319:    - the host writing the Command register; or
  1320:    - the host reading the Status register.
  1321: 
  1322: On PIO transfers, INTRQ is asserted at the beginning of each data block to be transferred. A data block is
  1323: typically a single sector, except when declared otherwise by use of the SET MULTIPLE MODE command.
  1324: An exception occurs on FORMAT TRACK, WRITE SECTOR(S), WRITE BUFFER and WRITE LONG
  1325: commands - INTRQ shall not be asserted at the beginning of the first data block to be transferred.
  1326: 
  1327: On DMA transfers, INTRQ is asserted only once, after the command has completed.
  1328: 
  1329: If the system shares this line with non-ATA devices, the ATA host or ATA adapter shall ensure that
  1330: appropriate protection is employed to protect ATA device INTRQ drivers from damage.
  1331: 
  1332: 5.2.11 IOCS16- (Device 16-bit I/O)
  1333: 
  1334: During PIO transfer modes 0, 1 or 2, IOCS16- indicates to the host system that the 16-bit data port has been
  1335: addressed and that the device is prepared to send or receive a 16-bit data word. This shall be an open
  1336: collector output.
  1337: 
  1338:           - When transferring in any PIO mode and accessing any register except the data port, transfers shall
  1339:           be 8-bit using DD0-7;
  1340:           - When transferring in PIO modes 0, 1 or 2, if IOCS16- is not asserted, transfers shall be 8-bit using
  1341:           DD0-7;
  1342:           - When transferring in PIO modes 0, 1 or 2, if IOCS16- is asserted, transfers shall be 16-bit using
  1343:           DD0-15;
  1344:           - When transferring in PIO modes 3 or 4, IOCS16- shall not be used by the host, and all transfers
  1345:           shall be 16-bit using DD0-15, except for bytes beyond the 512th byte for READ LONG and WRITE
  1346:           LONG commands which shall be 8-bit using DD0-7;
  1347:           - When transferring in DMA mode, the host shall use a 16-bit DMA channel and IOCS16- shall not
  1348:           be asserted.
  1349: 
  1350: 5.2.12 IORDY (I/O channel ready) (optional)
  1351: 
