  1519:     A     N              1       1      0                 * LBA bits 24-27             * LBA bits 24-27
  1520: 
  1521:     A     N              1       1      1                 Status                       Command
  1522: 
  1523:     A     A              x       x      x                 Invalid address              Invalid address
  1524: 
  1525:     * Mapping of registers in LBA mode
  1526: 
  1527:     Logic conventions are: A = signal asserted, N = signal negated, x = don't care
  1528: 
  1529:     Note 1 - This register is obsolete. It is recommended that a device not respond to a read of this
  1530:     address. If a device does respond, it shall be sure not to drive the DD7 signal to prevent possible
  1531:     conflict with floppy disk implementations.
  1532: 
  1533: 6.2.1 Alternate status register
  1534: 
  1535: This register contains the same information as the Status register in the command block. The only
  1536: difference being that reading this register does not imply interrupt acknowledge or clear a pending interrupt.
  1537: 
  1538:              7              6           54                3         2       1       0
  1539: 
  1540:                    BSY DRDY DF DSC DRQ CORR IDX ERR
  1541: 
  1542: See 6.2.12 for definitions of the bits in this register.
  1543: 
  1544: 6.2.2 Command register
  1545: 
  1546: This register contains the command code being sent to the device. Command execution begins immediately
  1547: after this register is written. The executable commands, the command codes, and the necessary parameters
  1548: for each command are listed in table 10.
  1549: 
  1550: 6.2.3 Cylinder high register
  1551: 
  1552: In CHS Mode, this register contains the high order bits of the starting cylinder address for any media access.
  1553: In LBA Mode, this register contains Bits 23-16 of the LBA for any media access.
  1554: 
  1555: This register shall be updated to reflect the media address of the error when a media access command is
  1556: unsuccessfully completed.
  1557: 
  1558: 16
  1559:                                                                              X3T10/0948D Revision 4c
  1560: 
  1561: NOTE 8 - Prior to the development of this standard, this register was updated at the end of every
  1562: media access command to reflect the current media address.
  1563: 
  1564: 6.2.4 Cylinder low register
  1565: 
  1566: In CHS Mode, this register contains the low order bits of the starting cylinder address for any media access.
  1567: In LBA Mode, this register contains Bits 15-8 of the LBA for any media access.
  1568: 
  1569: This register shall be updated to reflect the media address of the error when a media access command is
  1570: unsuccessfully completed.
  1571: 
  1572:      NOTE 9 - Prior to the development of this standard, this register was updated at the end of every
  1573:      media access command to reflect the current media address.
  1574: 
  1575: 6.2.5 Data register
  1576: 
  1577: The data register is either 8-bits or 16-bits depending on the interface width currently selected and/or the type
  1578: of data being transferred by the current command.
  1579: 
  1580: 6.2.6 Device control register
  1581: 
  1582: The bits in this register are as follows:
  1583: 
  1584:                             765432               1  0
  1585: 
  1586:                             r r r r r SRST nIEN 0
  1587: 
  1588: � Bits 7 through 3 are reserved;
  1589: � SRST is the host software reset bit. The device is held reset when this bit is set. If two devices are daisy
  1590: 
  1591:      chained on the interface, this bit resets both simultaneously;
  1592: 
  1593: � nIEN is the enable bit for the device interrupt to the host. When the nIEN bit is equal to zero, and the
  1594:      device is selected, INTRQ shall be enabled through a tri-state buffer. When the nIEN bit is equal to one,
  1595:      or the device is not selected, the INTRQ signal shall be in a high impedance state.
  1596: 
  1597: 6.2.7 Device/Head register
  1598: 
  1599: This register contains device addressing and sector addressing information.
  1600: 
  1601:                      7654                  3  2  1  0
  1602: 
  1603:                      r L r DEV HS3 HS2 HS1 HS0
  1604: 
  1605: � Bit 7 is reserved;
  1606: � L is the sector address mode select. When the L bit is equal to zero, addressing is by CHS mode.
  1607: 
  1608:      When the L bit is equal to one, addressing is by LBA mode;
  1609: 
  1610: � Bit 5 is reserved;
  1611: � DEV is the device address. When the DEV bit is equal to zero, Device 0 is selected. When the DEV bit
  1612: 
  1613:      is equal to one, Device 1 is selected;
  1614: 
  1615: � If the L bit is equal to zero (CHS Mode), the HS3 through HS0 bits contain the head address of the CHS
  1616:      address. The HS3 bit is the most significant bit. If the L bit is equal to one (LBA Mode), the HS3 through
  1617:      HS0 bits contain bits 27 through 24 of the LBA. This field shall be updated to reflect the media address of
  1618:      the error when a media access command is unsuccessfully completed.
  1619: 
  1620: NOTE 10 - Prior to the development of this standard, the head field (the HS3 through HS0 bits) of
  1621: this register was updated at the end of every media access command to reflect the current media
  1622: address.
  1623: 
  1624:                                                                                                    17
  1625: X3T10/0948D Revision 4c
  1626: 
  1627: 6.2.8 Error register
  1628: 
  1629: This register contains status from the last command executed by the device or a Diagnostic Code.
  1630: 
  1631: At the completion of any command except EXECUTE DEVICE DIAGNOSTIC, the contents of this register
  1632: are valid when the ERR bit is equal to one in the Status register.
  1633: 
  1634: Following a power on, a reset, or completion of an EXECUTE DEVICE DIAGNOSTIC command, this register
  1635: contains a diagnostic code (see table 11).
  1636: 
  1637:     76                        5  4             3  2  1  0
  1638: 
  1639:     r UNC MC IDNF MCR ABRT TKNONF AMNF
  1640: 
  1641: � Bit 7 is reserved;
  1642: � UNC (Uncorrectable Data Error) indicates an uncorrectable data error has been encountered;
  1643: � MC (Media Changed) is reserved for use by removable media devices and indicates that new media is
  1644: 
  1645:      available to the operating system. The MC bit shall be cleared by either a hardware reset or a power on
  1646:      reset.
  1647: 
  1648: � The first command following a media change shall be rejected with the MC bit set in the error register
  1649:      and the ERR bit set in the status register. The media changed state shall then be cleared and
  1650:      subsequent commands accepted normally;
  1651: 
  1652: � IDNF (ID Not Found) indicates the requested sector's ID field could not be found;
  1653: � ABRT (Aborted Command) indicates the requested command has been aborted because the command
  1654: 
  1655:      code is invalid or another device error has occurred;
  1656: 
  1657: � MCR (Media Change Requested) is reserved for use by removable media devices and indicates that a
  1658:      request for media removal has been detected by the device. When a request for media removal is
  1659:      detected, the MCR bit shall be returned in the error register and the ERR bit set in the status register for
  1660:      all subsequent DOOR LOCK commands. The MCR bit shall be cleared by a DOOR UNLOCK command,
  1661:      a MEDIA EJECT command or by a hard reset;
  1662: 
  1663: � TK0NF (Track 0 Not Found) indicates track 0 has not been found during a RECALIBRATE command;
  1664: � AMNF (Address Mark Not Found) indicates the data address mark has not been found after finding the
  1665: 
  1666:      correct ID field.
  1667: 
  1668: 6.2.9 Features register
  1669: 
  1670: This register is command specific and may be used to enable and disable features of the interface e.g.; by
  1671: the SET FEATURES Command to enable and disable caching.
  1672: 
  1673: This register may be ignored by some devices.
  1674: 
  1675: Some hosts, based on definitions prior to the completion of this standard, set values in this register to
  1676: designate a recommended Write Precompensation Cylinder value.
  1677: 
  1678: 6.2.10 Sector count register
  1679: 
  1680: This register contains the number of sectors of data requested to be transferred on a read or write operation
  1681: between the host and the device. If the value in this register is zero, a count of 256 sectors is specified.
  1682: 
  1683: For media access commands, this register shall be zero at the completion of a command if there is no error
  1684: indication in the Status register. For media access commands that complete with an error indication in the
  1685: Status register, this register contains the number of sectors which need to be transferred in order to complete
  1686: the request.
  1687: 
  1688: 18
  1689:                                                                                          X3T10/0948D Revision 4c
  1690: 
  1691: The contents of this register may be redefined on some commands, e.g.; INITIALIZE DEVICE
  1692: PARAMETERS or WRITE SAME commands.
  1693: 
  1694: 6.2.11 Sector number register
  1695: 
  1696: In CHS Mode, this register contains the starting sector number for any media access. In LBA Mode, this
  1697: register contains Bits 7-0 of the LBA for any media access. This register is used by some non-media access
  1698: commands to pass command specific information from the host to the device, or from the device to the host.
  1699: 
  1700: This register shall be updated to reflect the media address of the error when a media access command is
  1701: unsuccessfully completed.
  1702: 
  1703:      NOTE 11 - Prior to the development of this standard, this register was updated at the end of every
  1704:      media access command to reflect the current media address.
  1705: 
  1706: 6.2.12 Status register
  1707: 
  1708: This register contains the device status. The contents of this register are updated to reflect the current state
  1709: of the device and the progress of any command being executed by the device. When the BSY bit is equal to
  1710: zero, the other bits in this register are valid and the other Command Block register may contain meaningful
  1711: information. When the BSY bit is equal to one, no other bits in this register and all other Command Block
  1712: registers are not valid.
  1713: 
  1714:      NOTE 12 - Although host systems might be capable of generating read cycles shorter than the 400
  1715:      ns specified for status update following the last command or data cycle, host implementations should
  1716:      wait at least 400 ns before reading the status register to insure that the BSY bit is valid.
  1717: 
  1718: For devices that implement the Power Management features, the contents of the Status register and all other
  1719: Command Block registers are not valid while a device is in the Sleep mode.
  1720: 
  1721: If the host reads this register when an interrupt is pending, the interrupt is cleared.
  1722: 
  1723: 7  6  54  3  2  1  0
  1724: 
  1725: BSY DRDY DF DSC DRQ CORR IDX ERR
  1726: 
  1727: � BSY (Busy) is set whenever the device has control of the command Block Registers. When the BSY bit
  1728:      is equal to one, a write to a command block register by the host shall be ignored by the device.
  1729: 
  1730:                                                                                          19
  1731: X3T10/0948D Revision 4c
  1732: 
  1733:           The device shall not change the state of the DRQ bit unless the BSY bit is equal to one. When the
  1734:           last block of a PIO data in command has been transferred by the host, then the DRQ bit is cleared
  1735:           without the BSY bit being set.
  1736:           When the BSY bit equals zero, the device may only change the IDX, DRDY, DF, DSC and CORR
  1737:           bits in the Status register and the Data register. All of the other command block registers and bits
  1738:           within the Status register shall not be changed by the device.
  1739:           NOTE 13 - The assertion of CORR by the device while the BSY bit is set to zero might not be
  1740:           recognized by BIOS and drivers which sample status as soon as the BSY bit is equal to zero.
  1741:           The BSY bit shall be set by the device under the following circumstances:
