  1802: 7. General operational requirements
  1803: 
  1804: 7.1 Reset response
  1805: 
  1806: There are three types of reset in ATA. The following is a suggested method of classifying reset actions:
  1807: 
  1808: � Power On Reset: the device executes a series of electrical circuitry diagnostics, spins up the HDA, tests
  1809:      speed and other mechanical parametrics, and sets default values.
  1810: 
  1811: � Hardware Reset: the device executes a series of electrical circuitry diagnostics, and resets to default
  1812:      values.
  1813: 
  1814: � Software Reset: the device resets the interface circuitry according to the Set Features requirement (See
  1815:      8.24)
  1816: 
  1817: The power on reset, hardware reset, and software reset protocols are defined in 9.1 and 9.2.
  1818: 
  1819: 7.2 Sector addressing
  1820: 
  1821: All addressing of data sectors recorded on the device's media is by a logical sector address. The mapping of
  1822: logical sector addresses to the actual physical location of the data sector on the media is vendor specific.
  1823: 
  1824: An ATA device shall support at least one logical CHS translation mode known as the default translation
  1825: mode. The device shall enter this translation mode following a reset. A device may support other logical
  1826: translation modes and the host may use the INITIALIZE DEVICE PARAMETERS command to select the
  1827: default CHS mode or any of the other supported CHS modes. The default translation mode is described in
  1828: the Identify Device information. The current translation mode may also be described in the Identify Device
  1829: information.
  1830: 
  1831: A CHS address is made up of three fields: the sector address, the head number and the cylinder number.
  1832: Sectors are numbered from 1 to the maximum value allowed by the current CHS translation mode but can
  1833: not exceed 255. Heads are numbered from 0 to the maximum value allowed by the current CHS translation
  1834: 
  1835:                                                                                                                                               21
  1836: X3T10/0948D Revision 4c
  1837: 
  1838: mode but can not exceed 15. Cylinders are numbered from 0 to the maximum value allowed by the current
  1839: CHS translation mode but cannot exceed 65,535.
  1840: 
  1841: When the host selects a CHS translation mode using the INITIALIZE DEVICE PARAMETERS command, the
  1842: host requests the number of sectors per logical track and the number of heads per logical cylinder. The
  1843: device then computes the number of logical cylinders available in requested mode.
  1844: 
  1845: Sequential access to logical sectors shall be accomplished by treating the sector number as the least
  1846: significant portion of the logical sector address, the head number as the middle portion of the logical sector
  1847: address and the cylinder number as the most significant portion of the logical sector address.
  1848: 
  1849: A device may support LBA addressing. A device that supports LBA addressing indicates this in the Identify
  1850: Device information. A host shall not attempt to use LBA addressing unless the device indicates the mode is
  1851: supported.
  1852: 
  1853: A device shall not change the addressing method and shall return status information utilizing the addressing
  1854: method specified for the command.
  1855: 
  1856: If a device supports LBA addressing mode, then the following shall be supported by the device:
  1857: 
  1858:           a) The host may select either the currently selected CHS translation addressing or LBA addressing
  1859:           on a command-by-command basis by using the L bit in the Device/Head register;
  1860: 
  1861:           b) If LBA addressing is supported, then the device shall supported LBA addressing for all media
  1862:           access commands, except for the FORMAT TRACK command. Implementation of LBA addressing
  1863:           for the FORMAT TRACK command is vendor specific. The L bit of the Device/Head register shall be
  1864:           ignored for commands that do not access the media;
  1865: 
  1866:           c) Logical sectors on the device shall be linearly mapped with the first LBA addressed sector (sector
  1867:           0) being the same sector as the first logical CHS addressed sector (cylinder 0, head 0, sector 1).
  1868:           Irrespective of the logical CHS translation mode currently in effect, the LBA address of a given
  1869:           logical sector does not change. The following is always true:
  1870: 
  1871:           LBA = ( (cylinder * heads_per_cylinder + heads ) * sectors_per_track ) + sector - 1
  1872:              where heads_per_cylinder and sectors_per_track are the current translation mode values.
  1873: 
  1874: 7.3 Power management feature set
  1875: 
  1876: The power management feature set permits a host to modify the behavior of a device in a manner which
  1877: reduces the power required to operate. The power management feature set provides a set of commands and
  1878: a timer that enable a device to implement low power consumption modes. A device that implements the
